  "notes": "The Black Mountain College Museum + Arts Center, 120 College Street, Asheville: since September 2018 a 6,000 sq ft space (about 560 m2: galleries, library, archive) in a renovated three-storey brick commercial block on Pack Square Park, not the small storefront the first model took it for. Redrawn on the block's real footprint from OpenStreetMap's outlines (Overture Maps: the museum's building, 31 by 37 m, 1,170 m2, 10.9 m high, and the lower annex joined to its east side), north up, set in the plot at the museum's point — Asheville's street grid is turned about 13 degrees, so the block is too (the first model stood 12 m south of it and square to north: on 65% of the plot's building cells; this one stands on 84% and needs no shift: node scripts/fit_ground.js). Read from the Commons photograph of March 2023 (seen at 250 px; the larger sizes were refused): red brick, a pale string course over the shop fronts and a pale cornice, big glazed shop bays under a dark awning on College Street, tall first-floor windows, a row of arched windows on the top floor (drawn square), a flat roof behind a brick parapet with a little green copper roof at the corner. Across the street (about 12 m wide) Pack Square Park's lawn, its pavilion and trees; the street trees bare, as photographed in March. Guessed: the heights of the floors (three, to 10.9 m by the map data), the window rhythm on the sides not photographed, the annex's height (9.6 m), the brick colour. The museum's own rooms are inside the block's ground floor; where is not known."
